"""Expand one existing timing note; preserve the landing page and URL."""
import json,re
import expand_hold_period as page
page.SLUG='closing-before-peak-season-str'
page.TITLE='STR Closing Before Peak Season: Rush-Cost Worksheet'
page.H1='Should You Rush an STR Closing Before Peak Season?'
page.DESC='Compare recoverable peak nights with rush costs before buying an STR. Use a readiness register and delayed-opening case without weakening diligence.'
page.FAQ=[
('Does closing before peak season mean I can accept guests immediately?', 'No. Possession, buyer-specific permission, insurance, safety work and operating readiness must be established. A recorded deed or published listing does not establish all of those gates.'),
('Should I compare peak gross revenue with expedited setup costs?', 'Compare incremental contribution after relevant variable costs, then subtract distinct rush spending and earlier ownership carry. Include only nights the accelerated plan actually adds; do not annualize a few weekends.'),
('Can I assume the seller’s reservations fund the accelerated purchase?', 'No. Verify the authorized reservation handoff, who owes each obligation and who receives each payment. Fund upfront costs independently of unsupported bookings and later guest payouts.'),
('What if the property cannot open by the target weekend?', 'Recalculate fewer-night and no-extra-night cases. Seek a valid extension or revised funded plan with the relevant professionals; do not assume a right to delay, waive safety or consume the retained reserve without updating it.')]
page.BODY='''<p class="lead">Rush an STR closing before peak season only when the accelerated plan preserves diligence, passes every opening gate and creates enough supported incremental contribution to justify its extra costs. A closing date is not a first-stay date. For a buyer purchasing within zero to six months, four attractive weekends are a reason to test the schedule, not a reason to accept unresolved permission, inspection or financing questions.</p>
<p><a href="/apply/">Bring the candidate property, seasonal calendar and rush-cost comparison to an acquisition call</a>. This decision worksheet expands the original four-weekend example. It is not a booking forecast, a local permit timetable or a promise that an earlier closing improves the investment.</p>
<h2>Compare two complete acquisition plans</h2>
<p>Write a supported normal closing-and-opening plan and a proposed accelerated plan for the same property. Record price, financing, possession, required work, earliest lawful first stay and owner cash due by date. If acceleration changes the price or loan, include that difference rather than crediting the improvement entirely to seasonal revenue. Keep setup that both plans require outside the incremental comparison, but funded in the full purchase budget.</p>
<p>The <a href="/blog/str-purchase-to-launch-timeline/">purchase-to-launch guide</a> covers dependency scheduling and delay reserves. The <a href="/blog/new-listing-ramp-underwriting/">new-listing ramp worksheet</a> covers monthly receipts and cumulative cash draw. This page answers the narrower offer decision: is spending more or closing earlier to capture particular dates worth it, without surrendering necessary evidence?</p>
<h2>Use a dated opening-evidence register</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Evidence and responsible party</th><th>Acceleration limit</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Contract, loan and possession</td><td>Written settlement calendar, lender conditions and lawful access confirmed by transaction professionals</td><td>An advertised closing target is not cleared funding</td></tr>
<tr><td>Buyer permission and coverage</td><td>Applicable authority/association decision and effective insurance for intended use</td><td>A filed application or seller permit is not assumed buyer approval</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inspection and required work</td><td>Inspection findings, accepted repair scope and completion verification</td><td>Expedited delivery cannot cure unresolved safety work</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inventory and guest access</td><td>Installed furnishing, working utilities and tested arrival/turnover</td><td>A shipped order is not an installed, usable room</td></tr>
<tr><td>Listing and handoff</td><td>Authorized account setup, accurate photos, vendor coverage and reservation responsibilities</td><td>Published availability does not prove bookings or cash</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>For each row, enter a named owner, committed completion date, evidence still missing and fallback. The latest indispensable gate controls the first stay. Example only: possession on day 0, required work accepted day 5, furnishing installed day 8, buyer approval effective day 12 and tested turnover day 13 means day 13 is the earliest candidate opening in this invented sequence—not day 0. If approval slips to day 20, revise the dependent dates and recoverable nights. These are not standard lead times.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.airbnb.com/help/article/883">Airbnb’s publishing instructions</a> say a newly Listed home is usually visible within 24 hours but may take up to 72 hours to appear in guest search results. That platform guidance does not establish bookings, ranking, legal permission or payout timing. Include platform setup uncertainty without treating 72 hours as a guaranteed saleable launch.</p>
<h2>Worked four-weekend rush-cost comparison</h2>
<p>Hypothetical original arithmetic, not market data or a client outcome. Assume the accelerated plan could add eight booked nights that the normal plan would miss, at an invented $500 nightly lodging receipt. Assume relevant variable outflows equal 25% of those receipts, an incremental $1,800 expedited setup charge and $900 additional ownership carry caused by closing earlier. The $900 is not all annual fixed cost. Both plans’ common price, normal setup and later operating costs are excluded from this incremental screen, not from acquisition underwriting.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Extra nights actually achieved</th><th>Receipts less variable outflows</th><th>Less $2,700 incremental rush/carry</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Eight nights</td><td>$4,000 − $1,000 = $3,000</td><td>$300 incremental cash contribution</td></tr>
<tr><td>Four nights after a delay</td><td>$2,000 − $500 = $1,500</td><td>−$1,200</td></tr>
<tr><td>No additional nights</td><td>$0</td><td>−$2,700</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>In this simplified model each extra booked night contributes $375 before the distinct rush/carry costs. $2,700 divided by $375 is 7.2 nights; eight whole nights are needed for a positive result under these inputs. That is an arithmetic threshold, not evidence eight guests will book. If discounts, fees, turnover patterns or variable expenses differ, replace the assumed per-night contribution. Include separate cancellation or remedial-work exposure if supported; do not invent a probability or subtract a cost already in the variable allowance twice.</p>
<p>The eight-night case adds only $300, not $4,000 of profit. The delayed case loses incremental cash even though it has gross revenue. Neither figure measures total annual return or property value. A waived inspection issue of unknown size is not priced by the $300 surplus. Keep unresolved decisive diligence as a stop gate rather than assigning it an arbitrary allowance to make the model pass.</p>
<h2>Fund costs before counting guest receipts</h2>
<p>Put the expedited invoice, earlier ownership bills, normal acquisition/setup obligations and retained operating cushion on a dated cash schedule. If the extra $2,700 is payable before the first guest receipt, independently available cash must cover it. A positive later $300 comparison does not erase that initial funding need. Keep household and business commitments separate; do not describe a spent operating reserve as still retained.</p>
<p>For an occupied sale, the <a href="/blog/future-reservations-at-closing/">future-reservations guide</a> addresses guest obligations and payment ownership. Existing seller bookings are not automatically buyer receipts. Reconcile the authorized handoff before counting them as recoverable nights, and protect guest information through agreed secure channels.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/close/review-documents-before-closing/">CFPB closing-document checklist</a> recommends advance review and comparing final loan terms with the estimate. Its consumer-mortgage guidance describes a three-business-day Closing Disclosure review period for covered loans. Do not apply that rule universally to every business-purpose STR product; ask the lender and closing professional what applies to the actual transaction. Review time, funding conditions and verified wire instructions are not optional merely because a weekend is approaching.</p>
<h2>Make the offer decision with explicit stop conditions</h2>
<p>Proceed with acceleration only when documented readiness, supported additional nights and independently funded cash justify it. Reprice or remove rush spending when the narrower contribution case is weak. Request an extension through valid contractual processes if critical evidence is late; an extension or cancellation right is not assumed. Decline a rushed plan that requires unsupported bookings, sacrificed safety, misleading loan use or unfunded opening costs.</p>
<p><a href="/apply/">Review both closing-and-opening plans before committing capital</a>. BNB Accelerator assists within its contracted acquisition scope; lenders, counsel, insurers and local authorities determine their respective requirements. See the <a href="/blog/buy-str-high-income-large-tax-bill/">high-income purchase roadmap</a> for the broader decision path.</p>
<p class="small">Primary pages checked October 7, 2026; the CFPB page displays an August 2025 modification, not newly issued 2026 rules. Educational information, not personalized legal, lending, tax or investment advice. All dates, costs, nights and receipts in the examples are hypothetical. No booking, opening date, financing, tax benefit or investment return is guaranteed.</p>'''
if __name__=='__main__':
 page.main('2026-09-23','data-peak-season-review-link','Before paying to accelerate a closing, use the <a href="/blog/closing-before-peak-season-str/">peak-night contribution and rush-cost worksheet</a> with a delayed-opening case and dated readiness evidence.')
 p=page.ROOT/'blog'/page.SLUG/'index.html';s=p.read_text()
 s=re.sub(r'<span>Updated [^<]+</span>','<span>Updated October 7, 2026</span>',s,count=1)
 if '"@type": "FAQPage"' not in s:
  d={'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'FAQPage','mainEntity':[{'@type':'Question','name':q,'acceptedAnswer':{'@type':'Answer','text':a}} for q,a in page.FAQ]}
  s=s.replace('</head>','<script type="application/ld+json">'+json.dumps(d)+'</script>\n</head>',1)
 p.write_text(s)
